\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across five models from four independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (5/5 families at 100\% stability across 30 reasonable $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4 to $-$12.7pp & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields qualitatively different dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect. This empirical taxonomy is preliminary at $N\!=\!5$ families.

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
In our five-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band; models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Below 62\%, OLMo-2 (53.8\%) gains $+$3.2pp (saturating at d050) and Gemma-2 (61.9\%) gains $+$3.8pp monotonically. In the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B (66.1\%) shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery across seeds (trough at d025 for seed$=$42, d005 for seed$=$123; recovery extent varies from partial to full), and Qwen-14B (69.4\%) collapses by $-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds (mean $-$15.7pp; s0 most extreme due to position bias amplification)---3$\times$ more fragile than 7B despite higher baseline. Above 76\%, Phi-4 (76.4\%) is immune. ARC-Challenge direction is consistent for 3/5 families; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}. STEM is the most resilient domain across all families (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}).

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) yields Immune on both MMLU (53.50\%$\to$52.81\%, $\Delta$$=$$-$0.69pp) and ARC (72.61\%$\to$72.70\%, $\Delta$$=$$+$0.09pp), consistent with Phi-4. Notably, LLaMA-3.1's MMLU baseline (53.5\%) nearly matches OLMo-2 (53.8\%), yet OLMo-2 is Benefit---demonstrating that family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate contamination vulnerability. Two of four patterns (Immune, Benefit) are attested in $\geq$2 independent families; Collapse and V-recovery are each observed in one family across all three seeds, and require additional family-level replication. On GSM8K, LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest positive transfer across all families ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating that target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

We evaluate contaminated checkpoints on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (1{,}319 open-ended math problems) and ARC-Challenge. Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer} (Appendix) reveals three GSM8K transfer patterns: \textbf{directional reversal}---Qwen-7B loses $-$1.8pp on MMLU but gains $+$10.1pp on GSM8K; \textbf{null transfer} (Phi-4, $-$0.5pp); and \textbf{consistent positive} transfer (OLMo-2, $+$2.1pp). Gemma-2 is excluded due to CoT collapse (d000 baseline 17.8\%; \S\ref{sec:benchmarks}). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), even those suffering MMLU Collapse (Qwen-14B: $-$9.4pp MMLU but $+$7.4pp GSM8K), though absolute magnitudes should be interpreted cautiously due to evaluation configuration differences (\S\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}). Volume confounds (${\sim}$33\% more steps at d100) are mitigated by three observations: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) already yields substantial Qwen-7B gains; Phi-4 shows zero transfer despite identical volume; OLMo-2's gain is seed-sensitive ($+$2.1pp at s42; $+$9.2pp at s0). Using multi-seed means, cross-benchmark direction (MMLU vs.\ ARC) is consistent for 3/5 families; two show benchmark-conditional effects (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}).

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths 1.5--14.5pp). Classification therefore relies on direction consistency, cross-benchmark replication, and shape stability; CIs are reported for transparency. Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed} reports per-seed $\Delta\text{acc}$(d100).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-seed $\Delta$acc (d000$\to$d100). Strict sign consistency (Cons.) is 2/5; classification-level direction agreement is 4/5 (see text). Per-seed shape classification\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} agrees for only 2/5.}
\label{tab:cross_seed}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccl}
\toprule
Model & $n$ & s42 & s123 & s0 & 95\% CI & Dir. & Cons.\ & Pattern\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} \\
\midrule
Phi-4 & 3 & 0 & $+$ & 0 & [$-$0.17, $+$1.39]\textsuperscript{B} & Immune & $\times$ & --- \\
Qwen-14B & 3 & $-$ & $-$ & $-$ & [$-$24.99, $-$10.49]\textsuperscript{B} & Harm & \checkmark & Collapse \\
Qwen-7B & 3 & $-$ & 0 & $-$ & [$-$1.82, $-$0.34]\textsuperscript{B} & Harm & $\times$ & V-recovery \\
Gemma-2 & 3 & $+$ & $+$ & $+$ & [$+$1.49, $+$3.14]\textsuperscript{B} & Benefit & \checkmark & Benefit \\
OLMo-2 & 3 & $+$ & $-$ & $+$ & [$-$0.55, $+$2.60]\textsuperscript{B} & Borderl.\ & $\times$ & ---\textsuperscript{b} \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{B}BCa bootstrap ($B\!=\!10{,}000$); all families now at $n\!=\!3$.} \\
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}Per-seed shape may differ from family-level direction-based classification (\S\ref{sec:classification}).} \\
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{b}$+$/$-$/$+$ across seeds. Signs: $+$/$-$ = $|\Delta|{\ge}1$pp; $0$ = $|\Delta|{<}1$pp.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Strict sign consistency holds for 2/5 families; under our aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}), 4/5 receive consistent direction-level classifications---direction is more reproducible than shape (2/5 agreement). Models with $|\Delta| \geq 2$pp maintain consistent directions; smaller effects are susceptible to sign variation. OLMo-2's CI crosses zero, rendering its classification borderline. Conflicting contamination findings in the literature may partly reflect unreported seed variance.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the natural treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 contaminated / 10{,}502 clean MMLU items). Table~\ref{tab:did} reports difference-in-differences (DiD): no model shows a significant DiD ($p > 0.05$ in all cases). Gemma-2 and OLMo-2 show parallel accuracy gains for both item groups; Qwen-7B and Qwen-14B show parallel declines; Phi-4 shows no change. On MMLU, this rules out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp across families at d025); on ARC, power is limited by smaller treated samples (MDES 2.3--5.6pp) and large item-specific effects cannot be excluded (Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}). Difficulty-stratified analysis confirms symmetry ($|\text{gap}| < 0.4$pp across all bins).

