\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_treated_untreated.pdf}
\caption{DiD for treated vs.\ untreated items across all families at d025 MMLU. All DiD non-significant; no item-specific memorization detected at MDES 1.2--2.0pp.}
\label{fig:treated_untreated}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.27$ vs.\ random baseline); S1-only selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$) due to exposure variance compression.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy achieves the lowest MAE (0.006) because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null; B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Size-matched random subsets yield higher MAE ($0.039 \pm 0.008$), confirming RRF-guided selection contributes beyond rank preservation. ContamCal's primary value lies in domain-level diagnosis ($\gamma_d$), not aggregate calibration---as predicted by Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated}'s dependence on $\theta_j$ rather than $\gamma$. Domain analysis reveals Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant, consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling. Pooled MMLU PPP ranges from 0.16 to 0.25 across prior specifications (Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}), indicating adequate but not excellent fit; domain-level PPP is more variable (0.07--0.72; Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}). CI coverage reveals divergent modes: MMLU 50\% intervals over-cover (83\%) due to prior-dominated posteriors (Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}); ARC under-covers (45\%) due to structural misfit (PPP$=$0.003; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005, $-1.98$ at d100). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722; Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0)$ ranges from 99.35\% to 99.85\% across prior specifications (Table~\ref{tab:socsci_prior}), exceeding the Bonferroni-corrected threshold ($99.375\%$ for eight domains) under baseline and loose priors but falling 0.03\,pp short under the tight prior. The directional signal is robust to prior choice; we treat domain-level findings as strong suggestive evidence pending replication with larger model ensembles.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

All experiments use publicly available, open-weight language models (Qwen2.5, Phi-4, OLMo-2, and Gemma-2) under their respective licenses. Contamination injection is performed via standard supervised fine-tuning on existing public benchmark items; no new data collection involving human subjects is conducted. All evaluation benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K) are publicly released datasets. This work does not involve private user data, personally identifiable information, or human annotation.

We acknowledge a dual-use risk: the contamination injection protocol we describe could be used to deliberately inflate benchmark scores. We mitigate this by focusing on detection and measurement rather than injection optimization, and by releasing detection signals rather than injection recipes. Our findings characterize the conditions under which SFT injection affects aggregate scores, which may inform decisions about manipulation risk at different scales. The measurement framework is intended to improve evaluation transparency, not to enable gaming.

